If you use a BA23 box with a PDP-11/93, RD54 drive and 2 * RX31 floppy 
drives, that is not
much different than a very old Pentium in a case with an old ATA drive 
and a 5 1/4" floppy.
No blinkin lights and very little noise.  This will also run both SIMH 
and Ersatz-11, although
not as fast as a 3 GHz core 2 duo with SATA2 1 TB hard drives.  In the 
latter case, CPU
speeds of 100 times a PDP-11/93 are achieved.  Disk I/O speeds of 200 
times the RD54
are possible.

>I would like to solicit peoples opinion on strengths/weaknesses of
>these emulators (from your perspective).  My intent is to better
>understand their capabilities, not start a religious war.
>  
>
Other than the excessive noise and very heavy cabinets associated with 
the older PDP-11
systems that use RL02 and RK05 drives, I can't see why this would be a 
religious war.

Software addicts to the PDP-11 tend to use the Ersatz-11 solution since 
it is probably
about ten times the speed of SIMH.  When convenience, cost and 
production factors
are considered, Ersatz-11 wins all the time.  For example, Johnny solved 
the cost
problem of running RSX-11 by shifting from a real DEC PDP-11 with the 
high cost
of power to running Ersatz-11 on a PC.

Hardware addicts who enjoy fixing and restoring real DEC PDP-11 systems find
their enjoyment in running the actual original hardware.  Each to their 
own preference.

>Also, one question I have is: is either simulator capable of having
>actual hardware in the loop?  for example, a LA-120 or VT100,  or of
>  
>
You are asking two VERY different questions.  Under Ersatz-11 (and 
probably SIMH),
a serial port is a serial port.  Until I managed to acquire a video card 
and monitor which
would support 132 character text lines (under W95 on a Pentium), I used 
the COM1:
and COM2: ports to interface to Ersatz-11 and run a real DEC VT100 to 
support
looking at MACRO-11 listings with 132 character text lines.

IN  ADDITION, Ersatz-11 has software settings which support the use of a 
real DEC
TU-58 drive on that same serial line.  In fact, at one point I had the 
fun of enhancing
V05.04G of RT-11 to be Y2K compliant, then coping the required files to 
TU-58
media on a real DEC TU-58 drive from 1979 and booting RT-11.  The TU-58 
drive
and media were over 20 years old, and I was running a Y2K compliant 
version of
RT-11 on a PC (using Ersatz-11 under W95).  Booting and file access were 
just as
slow as always, of course.

If you don't need to use actual TU-58 tape media (for interchange or 
whatever purpose),
Ersatz-11 supports DD: emulation.  Your program will no know the 
difference, only
the speed of the access to the DD: device will be changed - probably 
around 1,000,000
times as fast.  OK just an estimate - maybe only 100,000 times as fast.

>immediate interest, a TU-58? Of course there would be the appropriate
>hardware interface adaptation, e.g. current loop to voltage, etc.
>  
>
The serial ports on the old PC were RS-232 and quite compatible with 
both the TU-58
and VT100.

The other aspect of your question is if actual Qbus and Unibus boards 
can be using along
with either SIMH or Ersatz-11.  The answer for SIMH is "I don't know", 
but probably
could be done if you have the required hardware and software expertise.

The answer for Ersatz-11 is YES!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!

A PCI board exists (with the cables able to plug into the PCI board at 
one end and
the Qbus or Unibus at the other end - for example a BA23 box in the case 
of the Qbus)
which is FULLY supported by Ersatz-11.  If a specific emulation is not 
supported
by Ersatz-11 and you absolutely must be able to still run the associated 
hardware
and software, then the commercial version of Ersatz-11 along with the 
already
mentioned PCI card (at an even higher cost) for those commercial 
situations which
require the 100 times the speed of a PDP-11/93 and the retention of special
purpose Qbus and / or Unibus boards.  Such systems are actively in use.

>You can also mail responses directly to me: jjhudak at gmail
>  
>
I though this answer might be of interest to everyone.

Jerome Fine


