> I would like to solicit peoples opinion on strengths/weaknesses of
> these emulators (from your perspective).  My intent is to better
> understand their capabilities, not start a religious war.
>
> Also, one question I have is: is either simulator capable of having
> actual hardware in the loop?  for example, a LA-120 or VT100,  or of
> immediate interest, a TU-58? Of course there would be the appropriate
> hardware interface adaptation, e.g. current loop to voltage, etc.
>
> You can also mail responses directly to me: jjhudak at gmail

Short answer on the actual hardware thing, since noone have answered 
that correctly yet, as far as I can tell.

Both E11 and simh can connect to ethernet, faking a DEC ethernet 
controller. Both can connect a telnet port to a serial port, making it 
possible to telnet to the simulator, and appear to be connected to a 
serial port on the simulator.
However, only E11 can connect a simulated serial port to an actual 
physical serial port. simh cannot do this. The same goes for any other 
direct mapping between a simulated device and a physical one. Most of 
the time, E11 can, and simh cannot.

Apart from this, both simulators work just fine, and both are equally 
usable. There are some odd extra stuff in E11 which don't exist in simh, 
and vice versa.

	Johnny

