On Feb 15, 9:57=A0am, Michael Kraemer <M.Krae...@gsi.de> wrote:
> John Wallace schrieb:
>
> > I could have sworn I'd seen Linux-86-hosted ARM-targetted gcc support
> > both little endian and big endian ARM.
>
> > Yes, here's a URL:http://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/ARM-Options.html
>
> > There are practical limitations in what can be done with gcc cross
> > compiles, and what is sensible to do, but the endianness issue doesn't
> > appear to be one of them.
>
> The architecture switches may be there, but for an executable you'd need
> the system libraries too. As it seems to be, Linux can't keep both,
> 32 and 64bit, on the same machine.
> gcc barfs when I want -m32 on 64bit and vice versa.
> Not so for AIX, for example. Or HP-UX. Didn't check the others, yet.
> But this starts to get OT for comp.sys.dec.

Add my place of work to the list of folks using gcc and friends on a
64bit (x86) Linux to cross compile for a 32bit embedded system.
There's even something called a Canadian cross too, e.g. where a Linux/
64 box used by the compiler developer builds a compiler for the system
engineers to use on mingw on Windows, still targeting the same 32bit
embedded system. Far too complicated, but it works.


