(and someone else wrote)
>> If one depended heavily on 64bit integer operations, that is.
>> Everybody else had 64bit FP anyway, and 64bit addresses
>> were only marginally useful since few systems could be stuffed
>> with so much RAM, even if one was rich enough.
 
> You are rather misunderstanding the difference between a 64bit virtual
> address and the ability to address more than 32bits of physical
> memory. But don't worry about it, plenty of others have made the same
