Lawrence D'Oliveiro <ldo@geek-central.gen.new_zealand> wrote:
> In message <ij8pdn$fpj$02$1@news.t-online.com>, Michael Kraemer wrote:
 
>> Everybody else had 64bit FP anyway ...
 
> H_FLOATING, anyone?

The IBM 360/85 had 128 bit floating point long before, along
with all models of S/370.  (Except that divide was done in software
emulation until late in the ESA/390 years.)
 
> One thing that mildly surprised me was that the Alpha kept the distinction 
> between integer and floating-point registers.

It makes it much easier for the processor to do out-of-order
execution if you don't have to keep track of which values are 
going where.  Also, many don't save the floating point registers
during task switches when it isn't needed.
 
> Combining the two sets would have given you a bit more flexibility, if 
> you???re running short of one kind and have an excess of the other kind.

Well, for one that means more instruction bits.  By keeping them
separate you get the appropriate number of each, addressed by
the appropriate instruction type.

-- glen


