May I ask *why* have you been running a full 64bit desktop?

This is a genuine open question. As Glenn H noted earlier (prompted by
Mr Knuth) what often happens when you do this is you waste a valuable
chunk of cache because addresses of data are twice as big as they need
to be, therefore there is an unnecessary performance hit.

So, do your applications simply not work in less than 33bits address
space? Do you have objective metrics that show your desktop runs
"better" (e.g. faster) in 64bit? Do you have a subjective metric that
it "feels snappier"? Or is it just "because it's there"? There are no
"wrong" answers to this question, btw. At work, I've just moved my
development environment from 32bit OS version 11.1 to 64bit OS 11.3 ie
changed two things at once (bad idea). After a bit of informal testing
I am not convinced there is any significant benefit for me in going to
64bit for the apps likely to be used locally (compilers etc for small
realtime programs) but the decision appears to be being dictated from
above for "future proofing", and there is probably something in that.


