>
> I remember (back in 198?) looking at the RK07 engineering prints for
> the new RK07 we just received and noticed the dual porting
> capability.  In talking with a colleague, who was our resident DEC
> expert, said too bad it is not supported in software.  I believe he
> was talking about RT and RSX 11, which we ran occasionally.  Since we
> were part of a university, we ran UNIX BSD 6 and later BSD 7 on our
> DEC machines.  UNIX didnt support dual porting either, because there
> were no drivers...so I ended up writing one, only to get about 90%
> finished until I had to work on paying customer projects.  So, my
> question is, did any DEC OS of the RT or RSX families support dual
> ported disks?

Yes, RSX supports dual-ported disks, and have done so for a long time. 
Definitely support by the mid-80s.

However, RSX did not support having two different systems connected to 
the same disk. But if you had two disk controllers on your system, you 
could have them both access the same disk.

The reason for this support was initially multiprocessor PDP-11 systems, 
where you could then have fault tolerance, and the ability to hotswap 
even controllers. It also meant that you could potentially have a short 
path to the disk from two different CPUs.
But it sometimes also made sense from a pure performance view. If you 
had two massbuses on the system (not unusual on a PDP-11/70), you could 
have your disk on both massbuses. That meant that if one massbus was 
busy with a transfer to some other device, you could still perform I/O 
to the disk via the second massbus.
Load sharing... But since you normally only had one Unibus, it was less 
of a point for something like the RK07. But since the structures in RSX 
already existed, it worked just as well for the RK disks.

