>
> I don't know about RT-11 or RSX-11 offhand. RSTS/E, though, could
> support SOME of the drives in dual-ported mode, but not all. The
> MASSBUS-interfaced drives, RM02/RM03/RM05/RM80, could be accessed by two
> systems simultaneously, but they must be mounted read-only. Other
> dual-ported drives (RK07, RAxx) weren't supported even in RO mode for
> simultaneous access in RSTS/E.
>
> It seems the dual-port feature in the DEC world was mainly a matter of
> having a spare system connected, so if the machine went down due to a
> hardware problem that didn't involve the drives, you could just hit the
> A/B switches and boot up the other system, with downtime dominated by
> the time it took to sprint down the hall to the machine room.

No.

Like I just replied in another post, for RSX the dual port feature was 
used for increased performance within one system, and also for more 
advanced stuff with multiprocessor systems.

For VMS, dual-porting was used much more extensively for fault tolerant 
clusters. If you wanted to have a system with no single point of 
failure, you needed the dual port feature.

You had two VAXen. The CI connection is a dual path with redundancy. You 
then setup two HSC disk controllers on the CI bus as well. And then your 
disks are connected to both HSCs, so both ports enabled. And of course, 
the disks are setup as Raid-1 (in modern speak). This is handled by the 
HSC controllers.

At this time, there is no single point of failure left.
(Aha, you say, but what about power - well, you are supposed to have the 
VAXen, HSCs and disks connected to different power feeds, to avoid that 
problem as well.)

	Johnny

