(snip)

> This is a genuine open question. As Glenn H noted earlier (prompted by
> Mr Knuth) what often happens when you do this is you waste a valuable
> chunk of cache because addresses of data are twice as big as they need
> to be, therefore there is an unnecessary performance hit.

In the case of x86-64, the question is complicated by the registers
that are available only in the 64 bit mode.  At least I believe
that is still true.  It would seem that systems could make the
registers available and still use 32 bit addressing, though.

And note that 32 bit IA32 allows for a 36 bit physical address bus, 
but with the complication that a 32 bit MMU is used.  If not for
the 32 bit MMU, the 45 bit virtual address space (16 bit 
segment selectors, minus the global/local and ring bits) would
be available, allowing one to address at least the 36 bit
physical address space.

-- glen


