Bob Koehler <koehler@eisner.nospam.encompasserve.org> wrote:
(snip, I wrote)

>> It is, but it complicates it if it has to keep track of the
>> registers in both fixed and floating point sense.
 
> 
>   So get an Itanium, and start keeping track of register banks.

Well, the other choise is a stack.  The idea for the 8087 was
a virtual stack, where on overflow an interrupt routine would 
write some to memory, and on underflow restore them.  That avoids
the complication of keeping track of which routine needs how many
registers.  The problem for the 8087 was that no-one tried to write
the interrupt routine before the silicon was in production, and
then it turned out not to be possible.  The appropriate mode bits
weren't available, though I don't remember the details.
 
>   Then remember the PDP-1 through -8 (probably some others), where
>   the one accumulator was the only register you could actually use for
>   much.

In the days when instruction counting was useful and MIPS 
was a useful measure of processor speed.  Though many early
processors had the MQ register to keep the bits needed for
multiply and divide, even when implemented in software.

-- glen


