> platform, it would have been very unwise, commercially to let Tru64
> take precedence.
> Not that the DEC culture would have ever allowed that to happen.
> Hans

"In 1983 VMS was DEC's flagship OS and all engineering effort at DEC
was spent on VMS."

VAX Ultrix was released in mid 1984, so it's probably not entirely
fair to say that *all* engineering effort was spent on VMS in 1983.
But I'd probably accept "almost all".

One of my former colleagues worked on what was said to be the first
VAX Ultrix machine in the UK. It was delivered to HP Labs in Bristol,
in 1985 or thereabouts.


