 >Bob Koehler wrote:

>In article, Hans Vlems <hvlems@freenet.de> writes:
>  
>
>>Unlikely. In 1983 VMS was DEC's flagship OS and all engineering effort
>>at DEC was spent on VMS.
>>
>Maybe by 1993, but in 1983 RSX-11M/M+ and TOPS-20 were still going
>strong.
>  
>
Likewise for RT-11.  Between 1983 and 1992, DEC released 16 
distributions of V05.0n
for RT-11 beginning with V05.00 and ending with V05.06 which was ONLY 
partially
Y2K compliant.  Mentec released V05.07 in 1998 which supports the year 
from 1972
to 2099 as four digit years in most programs, but not all.

For V05.04 of RT-11, there were SEVEN sub-versions from V05.04A to V05.04G
followed by V05.05 of RT-11 in 1989 which substantially improved support 
for large
disk drives.  In 1992, V05.06 was released which finally provided 
support for I/D space.

Those 2 enhancements stand out in my mind (although I have never used 
I/D space)
along with probably hundreds of others, some very small, but others just 
as important.
Of course, there are still many bugs left, mostly undocumented, but 
which are probably
rarely encountered.  And there are still many enhancements waiting to be 
released by
hobby users.

One key point for those users who are software addicts (as opposed to 
the hardware
type of addict who mostly enjoys restoring old DEC PDP-11 hardware), it 
is now
possible to run all three of the major PDP-11 operating systems using 
emulators.

For hobby users, SIMH and Ersatz-11 are being used to run RSTS/E, RSX-11
and RT-11 all the time, although for serious users, Ersatz-11 runs so 
much faster
than SIMH that there is really no other choice.  Recent CPU speeds on a 
core 2
duo under Windows XP are about 100 times the speed of a PDP-11/93.  For
disk IO, a conservative estimate is about 200 times a standard SCSI hard 
drive on
either Qbus or Unibus host adapters when SATA 3 hard disk drives are used.

One other VERY interesting aspect of Ersatz-11 is that it is possible to 
directly
access more than a GigaByte of the PC memory using  4  PDP-11  IOPAGE
registers.  The last two registers are used as a 32 bit byte address of 
the PC
memory which can be requested via the Ersatz-11 command:
INSTALL  dev:\path\EMEM  /SIZE:1024MB
Assuming that the address of the PC memory is already loaded in the last 2
IOPAGE registers, sequential (using the second register to access the PC
memory automatically increments the word address) access speeds take
only about 3 times as long as a normal PDP-11 memory access, mostly due
to the extra time by Ersatz-11 to handle the IOPAGE register reference
and pass the get / put access through to a separate DLL.  There are FORTRAN
subroutines available (IV and 77) to interface for the user who does not
use assembly language.

I have been unable to find anyone else still writing code to be used 
under RT-11
these days, but if you are out there, maybe change this thread to a PDP-11
reference and we can discuss it some more.

Jerome Fine


