On 2/11/11 6:21 AM, Michael Kraemer wrote:
>> and DEC to the
>> computing world as it is today
>
> and how much of that is still visible/important in today's IT at large?

   A good bit.  Ignoring general processes and methodologies and 
concentrating on specifics, here are a few examples:

   SAN technology is important.  I haven't researched their history, but 
CI-based storage sharing is the first SAN technology I ever used, around 
1988.  The capability was announced in 1983.

   Ethernet is important.  DEC was involved in an early phase of the 
Ethernet standardization process (DIX, if you recall), and arguably is 
the company that pushed it into commercial viability.

   Lotus Notes is important.  Though it didn't officially originate at 
DEC, one of its early developers came from DEC, where he was a developer 
of Notes-11, a very early workgroup software system.

   64-bit computing is something the PC world thinks it invented 
relatively recently, but the first 64-bit DEC Alpha EV4 (21064) based 
systems shipped in 1992, and were quite mainstream in the high-end 
workstation world.  This is not to say that DEC somehow "invented" 
64-bit computing, but they did ship the first mainstream 64-bit 
microprocessors, and it took more than a decade for the x86 crowd to 
catch up.

   Speaking of Alphas, if memory serves, the AMD Athlon uses the 
front-side bus design of one of the Alphas, either the EV5 (21164) or 
the EV6 (21264).

   DLT tape and its descendants are important.  It traces its lineage 
back to the DEC TK50, circa 1984.  Many people see TK50 tapes around my 
house and ask "hey, why are those DLT tapes grey?"

   DEC sponsored (with money and people) MIT's Project Athena, which 
churned out the X Window system, which is used by everything except 
Windows and OS X.

   I'm sure there are other examples, but these are just off the top of 
my head.

               -Dave

-- 
Dave McGuire
Port Charlotte, FL



