On 2/11/11 1:42 PM, glen herrmannsfeldt wrote:
>>    SAN technology is important.  I haven't researched their history, but
>> CI-based storage sharing is the first SAN technology I ever used, around
>> 1988.  The capability was announced in 1983.
>
> I don't understand CI so well, but IBM had disk drives that could
> interface to more than one channel (and more than one computer)
> at least back to the S/360 days.  I believe a system has to do
> some kind of LOCK channel command before, and UNLOCK after to
> avoid problems.  (Consider the interleave of SEEK and WRITE commands.)

   Yes, dual-port drives.  DEC had those too, much earlier than CI, and 
I'm sure other companies did as well.  I have a few RK07 drives; they 
are dual-ported.

   CI is much more than that; it's a full-blown clustering interface. 
In fact, it was common to connect RA81 drives to HSC-xx CI-interfaced 
storage controllers, and the drives themselves were dual-ported.  One 
could have fully redundant paths to the drives.

   It's Good Stuff(tm).

