On 2/12/11 11:25 AM, jjh wrote:
>>     Yes, dual-port drives.  DEC had those too, much earlier than CI, and
>> I'm sure other companies did as well.  I have a few RK07 drives; they
>> are dual-ported.
>> ...
>
> I remember (back in 198?) looking at the RK07 engineering prints for
> the new RK07 we just received and noticed the dual porting
> capability.  In talking with a colleague, who was our resident DEC
> expert, said too bad it is not supported in software.  I believe he
> was talking about RT and RSX 11, which we ran occasionally.  Since we
> were part of a university, we ran UNIX BSD 6 and later BSD 7 on our
> DEC machines.  UNIX didnt support dual porting either, because there
> were no drivers...so I ended up writing one, only to get about 90%
> finished until I had to work on paying customer projects.  So, my
> question is, did any DEC OS of the RT or RSX families support dual
> ported disks?
>
> (We later got an RA80 that was supported when we got our vax 750,
> IIRC)

   I don't know about RT-11 or RSX-11 offhand.  RSTS/E, though, could 
support SOME of the drives in dual-ported mode, but not all.  The 
MASSBUS-interfaced drives, RM02/RM03/RM05/RM80, could be accessed by two 
systems simultaneously, but they must be mounted read-only.  Other 
dual-ported drives (RK07, RAxx) weren't supported even in RO mode for 
simultaneous access in RSTS/E.

   It seems the dual-port feature in the DEC world was mainly a matter 
of having a spare system connected, so if the machine went down due to a 
hardware problem that didn't involve the drives, you could just hit the 
A/B switches and boot up the other system, with downtime dominated by 
the time it took to sprint down the hall to the machine room.

               -Dave

