jjh <jjhudak@gmail.com> wrote:
> Is there a disk controller for a PDP 8/E that will interface to a
> RL02?

I pinged Charles Lasner on this question, and in general he welcomes me to
forward information of interest to this group.  Attached are his notes on RL
interfaces.  BTW, he has grappled with some health issues over the last
several months, but is currently enjoying fairly good health.

Regards,
Andy Valencia

> I will give an appropriate response to this subject.  It raises a
> bunch of issues all at once:
> 
> 1)  There are only two PDP-8-relevant interfaces for RL01/02 drives:
> 
> a)  The M8433 hex board; thus unsuitable for standard/unmodified quad
> Omnibus boxes.  [Note:  There are a few modified systems.  this
> involves removing the power supply to make room for the wider cards.
> This should not be confused with support for more than 32K memory
> options which requires a hex-wide box WITH CONNECTORS, although there
> are only a few signals; 0 signals supported means no KT8A or related
> stuff.  I can document an experimental, but functional project that
> was peformed to accomplish this with the intention of making a Lab-8/e
> compatible with the MDC8, also hex wide and mem-signals missing and
> 32K memory only.]
> 
> b)  The RL278 option for the DECmate I.  I have heard of at least one
> system so configured, but they clearly are rare.  [Note: I have the
> extremely rare quad pedestal RX02 drives desk, but it is easy to make
> any DM I like a pair of RX01/02 drives; all you need is some pretty
> easy to get/make cables.  I have also used such a configuration for
> the DM II RX78 option board to make an eight drive DM II: 4 RX50 as
> two pair and an RX01 and an RX02 all at the same time.  Totally
> supported by various software for the DM II including CP/M-80 and
> MS-DOS.  This is why there are MS-DOS machines where the convention
> for the hard disk is starting at E:, not C: to reserve 4 drives for
> floppies, not two as is common.]
> 
> I do not know if the RL278 supports the magic jumper [see below].
> 
> The RL disks were in many ways a dismal failure due to not getting the
> job done and instead obsessing on a worthy detail and ignoring the
> real problems created by bad overall design, which includes totally
> forgetting the design traditions of the immediate past.  Let me place
> disk controller architecture into proper scope to illustrate:
> 
> 1)  The base design for all that-era disks should start with the
> RK01/RK08.  This was a design that was about as good as it could get,
> especially in the light of it having to be implemented as a giant
> positive bus peripheral out of so many small modules.  But here is
> what you get that is really good:
> 
> a)  A real word count/current address DMA transfer.  Since the disk
> format is inherently 256 12-bit words/sector, OS/8 required the
> ability to set the word count to an ODD count in terms of total number
> of words transferred to the nearest 128, not 256.  [This has come up
> before:  OS/8, unlike P?S/8 requires the ability to read the first
> half of the sector; whatever is written when only the first half is
> meaningful isn't important.  However, it is crucial to  be able to
> support read transfers of the first half only.  On this hardware, this
> was a no-brainer.]
> 
> b)  You just ask for a physical starting sector/track.  The controller
> does it all, expecting you to only check status in case an error
> happened that causes a mis-seek or other error, not actually
> participate in the real-time aspects of the process.
> 
> If DEC had just LEFT THIS IN THE DESIGN, the RL disks would have been
> much more successful, including on the -11 family.
> 
> c)  The word count transferred as much as you wanted to; that more
> sectors got involved wasn't an issue; the controller just did it.
> 
> Please note that the drives for RK01 require maintenance in that the
> heads must be aligned using a standard alignment cartridge to make
> sure you are interchangeble to the standard; else all your packs are
> only readable on your drive, and worse, should you align it to the
> standard, you probably no longer can read your own packs!  Any
> realignment operations have to be coordinated with a data backup
> restore operation for all relevant data; if field circus did it
> without telling you, a lot of angry people is the result!
> 
> Simultaneously, we should track the software efficacy, particularly
> with regard to OS/8 handlers, both actual, and theoretical if there
> seems to be a possible improvement/trade-off.  [The tradeoff issue is
> that when the handlers were written, they had to fit in one page and
> the machine had to be 8K.  Later, a kludge was added upping the
> requirement to 12K, and the handler could be nearly two pages, with a
> lot of cross-field overhead, thus it effectively added say .9 of a
> second page.] [Note: P?S/8 uses a totally different memory model that
> is by nature uncompromising in the drive support issues; the raw space
> available is over 4 times as much code space as OS/8.]
> 
> I would give the RK01/08 OS/8 handler a B+.  It could be made just
> about an A rating if you require 12K and a rewrite.
> 
> The next generation is the RK05 and related family, and various Diablo
> drives used on the -11, some in-house only.  They are all pretty much
> the same varying only in specific configuration, and perhaps removable
> cartridges, etc.
> 
> The data rate is higher due to better density, and some have closer
> track spacing to get double the data out of the same real estate on
> the spinning disk, etc.
> 
> Various -11 and a single -8/e controller should work with the entire
> family, other than some nitpicking issues regarding extended drive
> support.  [Note: The "bare" RK8E controller uses individualized drive
> select, thus can have a maximum of 4 drives.  There is a modification
> to allow drive select by encoding a three-bit register, thus allowing
> 8 drives.  Please note than an RK05F counts as two drives of the total
> available.  Various -11 controllers support this in both modes.]
> 
> The PDP-8 RK8E interface is slightly compromised compared to the RK08,
> but is only a few Omnibus cards.  The compromises are:
> 
> i)  You can only transfer a single sector by DMA; you must initiate
> the next transfer in 100 microseconds to avoid a rotational latency,
> but other than the lost performance, the transfer will nominally
> succeed.
> 
> ii)  Software must determine whether to set a "don't check" bit for
> subsequent transfers.  If a single operation transfers sequential
> sectors on the same track, it is not possible to access the very next
> sector unless the don't check bit is set.  The reason is that checking
> means to check any sector encountered to see if on the expected track;
> that invalidates that particular sector, and then you have to wait
> until the relevant sector comes around to perform the data transfer.
> For logically linked sequential transfers, it is thus necessary to
> remove the checking on all subsequent transfers or else the check is
> performed on the sector needed to transfer, guaranteeing there will be
> a full revolution latency for each sector transferred, which on a
> standard PDP-8 16-sector disk cartridge it means transfers will run 16
> times as slow as expected!
> 
> However, reasonably written software can maintain the expected data
> rate; with interrupts off, as in say an OS/8 handler or whatever, it
> doesn't take 100 microseconds to get the second transfer going unless
> interrupted, such as when running F4.
> 
> To make OS/8 possible, a "half" bit was added to limit the DMA
> transfer to 128 words instead of the normal 256.  Thus, that bare
> aspect of what is done in the RK01/RK08 is maintained, etc.
> 
> Other than this, all other aspects of the RK01/08 is maintained,
> especially the notion of automatic seeking, the norm of the industy
> [and used by EVERYTHING EXCEPT THE RL DISKS!].
> 
> I have to rate the OS/8 handler a B-:
> 
> i)  Due to the attempt to make it fit in one page, the system handler
> is compromised in that the error handling is atrocious.  In essence,
> the worst-case error recovery operation is always performed regardless
> of what error it actually was.   This leads to poor performance on
> marginal disks, and I have personally demonstrated that sometimes you
> literally cannot recover the contents of a marginal disk using OS/8
> while using the P?S/8 BLKCPY program with its polar opposite stellar
> error recovery does in fact recover the data!   This seems to stem
> from a minor defect in the drive's power supply design:   Assume a
> parity data error is the actual problem [likely on a
> marginal/dirty/out-of-alignment pack]:  Since the OS/8 handler
> recalibrates the disk, which takes several seconds on any error, all
> of this waste of time occurs on this read parity data error which did
> NOT involve cylinder seek problems at all.  Apparently, this is a load
> on the power supply, and after the drive recovers, it needs a little
> more time to get back to voltage sufficiently to try again.  In a
> minute way, apparently this also means there is a small misalignment
> drift that wouldn't apply were it not for the disruptive recal
> operation.  Thus, it is possible to attempt to re-read and never get
> there, because you cannot prevent the recals.  In the P?S/8 scenario,
> there is an intense flurry of data read retries without any other
> operations; eventually the data reads without error and the copying
> can continue.  [This is an inherent feature of the BLKCPY program:  It
> attempts to transfer maximally per buffer unless there is an error; in
> that case, it transfers only until the latest logical call is
> satisfied.  At each level, there are retry counters to avoid excessive
> slowdowns.  But on marginal drives, it was repeatedly demonstrated
> this was the only way to successfully recover the data.]
> 
> ii)  As I explained above, this is one of those handlers written in
> the era where the 12K handler did not exist.  As a result there is
> also another problem besides the error-handling problem:  Due to
> unfixable design error, cylinder checking occurs twice as often as
> necessary thus slowing down the maximum transfer rate!  [I suspect
> this stems from a misunderstanding of the physical design of the drive
> on the part of ignorant programmers in DEC, most of whom I personally
> know.  For seek purposes, you must always set that check bit whenever
> you move the head to a new track AND for any initial transfer.
> However, the OS/8 handler sets the check bit when any SURFACE is
> changed, not when any CYLINDER is changed.  Surfaces change twice as
> fast as cylinders as there is an upper and a lower head on each
> cylinder.
> 
> In 12K both of these problems could be fixed, but the existing code
> has the limitations described.
> 
> There is a variant board set known as the RK8E-S.  It makes
> modifications to the existing cards and adds an additional card marked
> "RK8E Word Count Module".  It does a couple of things:
> 
> 1)  It sets a real word count register to resemble the RK01/08 in this
> regard.   This allows reliable real-time data transfers without either
> having horrible throughput when you fail that 100 microsecond window
> to get the next transfer going; the transfers are just automatic up to
> 4096 words/transfer; the "half" bit must never be set when this mode
> is enabled!
> 
> 2)  It makes the disk drive support the common -11 mode of an encoded
> register, thus up to 8 drives; an extra bit is added to become the
> high-order bit of the three in conjunction with the existing
> compatible two, but it is a disjoint instruction operation to access
> it; the code wouldn't be shorter, rather slightly larger overall.  But
> this was done for a customer that was unhappy with the real-time
> performance and didn't want to resort to 2:1 interleave as a
> guaranteed functional compromise to safely transfer at half-speed.
> 
> It is totally compatible with the original RK8E, as long as you ignore
> the potential of more than 4 drives.
> 
> A word on the drives themselves:
> 
> Up to this point, we are talking about alignment-requiring standard
> density removable cartridges, although the standard for the RK05 gets
> you twice as much data as the original Memorex-based RK01 [which in
> turn is pretty much the original IBM 1130 "pizza" disk].
> 
> The RK05F is a modification to double the track density thus getting 5
> MB per cartridge.  However, DEC was worried about general data safety
> and dirt implications and worse, inter-drive compatibility due to
> track alignment issues which are now much more stringent.  Thus, an
> RK05K pack removes all of the door and removal parts of the drive and
> adds a fixed mount to open the drive/head door on the cartridge;
> literally, you have to partially disassemble the drive to change the
> cartridge.  If you are careful, scrupulously align the heads, etc. it
> would not have been necessary to restrict the drive to a single
> cartridge formatted on that drive.  [Note:  A part of this has to do
> with bad field circus practice with regard to head alignment:  The
> common practice was to SLOPPILY not completely tighten the head
> alignment screws.  This causes two related issues:  The alignment is
> always suspect to being "a little off" and indeed cause many
> interchangebility issues, and of course a whole lot more "job
> security" for field circus itself.  I successfully personally
> eliminated this concern for ANY RK drive:  When you tighten the
> head-lock screw sufficiently AS DOCUMENTED as you achieve the proper
> tightness, the effective head position will predictably move and thus
> it winds up not where you want it.  However, you can do this once, and
> notice a rule-of-thumb of just how far off and in which direction it
> tends to drift off to.  Then you loosen it, and adjust it to being the
> same amount "off" in the opposite direction.  Thus, when you tighten
> the screw properly, the head moves to where you want it in the end!
> [This takes a little practice and experience to see the "jump factor
> for any particular head clamp, and compensate accordingly.]
> 
> Thus, it's a slipperly slope that interchangeablility in standard
> RK05K packs was marginal at best in terms of the typical sloppy field
> circus maintenance.  All of the drives I personally maintained the
> alignment lasted a whole lot longer, and interchangeability was not an
> issue.  By modifying an RK05F drive to use the J door and other
> removal parts, you could make a removable F pack system.  With only a
> modicum of cleanliness, and of course the mandatory use of my
> technique on the heads, you created an F pack interchangeablility
> standard that slightly exceeded the effective one [with field circus
> in the loop expected] for the normal J packs.
> 
> In the third-party world of Diablo and Western Dynex, this was
> expected value.  DEC was [properly so] looked upon as backwards.  I
> was involved with the DSD-240 hooked to a Western Dynex drive 5.0 MB
> fixed and 5.0 MB removable top cartridge.  [Note: RL packs resemble
> this drive; yes, said the right way since the RL disks came later!]
> 
> Because this is a half-removable 10 MB total pack, you can copy disks
> using the long way around techniques sometimes described as the "tower
> of hanoi" method:  If you want to copy a loose cartridge, first get
> out a designated temporary cartridge; spin up the top disk with the
> temp cartridge and do a 100% copy and verify the bottom disk onto the
> temp disk.  [Note: P?S/8 BLKCPY is ideal for this purpose; nothing can
> do it faster and it can be totally automated because the utility takes
> input control scripts from within a file, and in turn the entire thing
> can run under P?S/8 BATCH, all in 4K or more; 12K or more for maximum
> performance/minimum copying time.]
> 
> Then mount the disk to be copied; write it out on the bottom disk thus
> destroying the original bottom [which is saved on the temp disk!].
> Spin down, and place in the destination for the disk to be copied.
> Copy the entire bottom to the entire top; almost done.
> 
> Spin down and mount the temp disk; copy the top entirely to the bottom
> thus restoring the machine back to normal.  Try doing this on a single
> RK05 system! [which is in turn 2.5 MB; these cartridges and the bottom
> are each 5.0 MB.]
> 
> Thus, we have something even better than a modified RK05F disk; same
> capacity, take for granted the interchangeable 5.0 MB cartridges, and
> we get 10 MB with only one drive.  [You could hook up 4 such drives,
> but rarely was this done.]
> 
> I also participated in DSD modifying their controller in an analogous
> way to how DEC modified the RK8E to become the RK8F [to be put into
> the DW8E level converter for older machines].  One of the problems was
> SLOWING down the disk, which was inherently faster than the RK05 in
> terms of not only seek, size, but data rate.
> 
> The controller was a programmer's dream, as it was totally DMA,
> including DMA of the command to initiate the process.  The PDP-8 is
> status check and/or interruptible after the controller just does
> everything, and informs if there was/was not any errors.  This
> controller was part of the design inspiration for the MDC8.
> 
> Most importantly, yet another controller with automatic seeking.
> 
> The OS/8 [and P?S/8] handlers get an A; in a single page, the system
> handler has no compromises whatsoever.  The P?S/8 system handler runs
> on a 4K machine and accesses the entire disk top or bottom.  OS/8 has
> 4 handlers to access the top and 4 more to access the bottom.  I
> typically had P?S/8 on the fixed disk and booted OS/8 on a removable
> pack  and a production pack for P?S/8 on another removable disk.
> [Note: P?S/8 BLKCPY runs totally in memory once started; this means it
> was trivial to initiate a disk copy/backup operation without even
> booting to anything else.]
> 
> Thus, at this point, DEC introduces the RL disks, and they are a
> laughing stock in the industry, despite having a valid and important
> step forward [because in all the other ways that matter, it is several
> steps backwards!]  [If only DEC had lived up to its own standards on
> everything else!].
> 
> The good part about the RL disk is that there is no static head
> alignment per se.  Head positioning is always determined by a reticule
> mounted within each disk pack.  The servo system for any drive in good
> working order will always reproduce the proper head position within a
> very small and acceptable window.  This has the effect of making the
> drive cheaper to get to the same place as everyone outside of DEC had
> already gotten to, but since DEC always adds intrinsic overhead, they
> still cannot beat the competition on bottom-line price.  [And the
> competitors could always use imbedded servo design if they wish, and
> make DEC look more foolish!]
> 
> In any case, it is a good technology, and was used in a whole lot of
> other media over the years including devices such as the Imation
> LS-120 [not to be confused with a DEC printer of similar name!] which
> essentially writes on a drive equipped with a 1.44 MB diskette or a
> 120 MB reticule-controlled diskette-type flexible disk.  For the
> diskettes, it follows standard alignment, but for the high-density
> part it uses the media's individual reticule for proper alignment,
> etc.
> 
> The problem with the RL drives is that the rest of the controller is
> several steps totally backwards!  Arguably, this had more impact in
> the -11 world than in the -8 world; the standard -8 software is
> interrupts-off.  Interrupt-driven programs can be customized as
> necessary, and can include foreground "forks" if need be.  But on the
> -11, the interrupt-driven software was not expecting to require that,
> and thus had to be modified after the fact to handle an unexpected
> limitation.
> 
> In essence, the software has to initiate a seek operation IN SOFTWARE.
>  [More later on this.]  The status of the heads-are-in-motion must be
> monitored in real-time until the heads are again stable to advance to
> the next state of transfer.  To avoid a lot of lost throughput, this
> needs to be a foreground task, and can be initiated as part of a
> clock-interrupt routine to store the motion/no motion status to
> whichever calling program needs to know.  There is no interrupt when
> seek done or anything like that, just a head moving or not sense bit!
> 
> In PDP-8 non-interrupt usage, such as in OS/8 handlers with
> pathetically limited code space, this merely adds overhead as the bit
> is continuously monitored until stablely no longer moving.  Additional
> overhead is to then check to see if there was or was not a seek error
> in the attempt.
> 
> However, there is overhead in that the seek was initiated by the
> program and NOT the hardware; the software is responsible for all of
> the housekeeping to know when and how much and in which direction to
> reseek the heads until the desired position is obtained, and also to
> check to make sure that it worked as intended, else initiate recal
> operations and try again, etc.  Compare this to all of the above where
> you just check when it's done to see if you got a cylinder seek error
> or not.
> 
> And of course, the notion of secondary logical transfer has to be
> invented by the software, since there is no hardware "do not check
> bit".  The software has to determine if a subsequent transfer should
> proceed with or without first checking if another sector should be
> used to ensure being on the proper track, to be avoided to thus not
> contribute to the overall sloth due to rotational latency.
> 
> But in OS/8, there was no room to get any of this to work in any
> particularly good fashion, thus RL performance really sucks under
> OS/8.  The problems just interact badly leading to an awful kludge.
> [Many are familiar with my various positions on the poor design of
> OS/8 memory model system handlers and the obvious and predictable
> consequences.  The RL handlers represent the quintessential example of
> what I am complaining about; no third-party ever dared to offer
> something to customers this awful!].
> 
> Thus, the organization of the disk transfers in OS/8 is the following
> lame and pathetic notion:
> 
> Consider the disk to be organized into "wedges".  I forget the exact
> numbers, but on an RL01 you get 2 full-sized wedges and 1 half-sized
> wedges, each to be used as a separate logical device.  On the RL02
> double that to get 5 full-sized wedges.
> 
> RL disks are also [gasp!] not ERROR-FREE!  [All the ones above are!]
> It is necessary to honor the bad-block table of the media.  However,
> that is written out in a "foreign" area of the disk, and must be
> managed in 8-bit mode just to read the data.  As a result, a utility
> was written to "translate" the errors into a table that can be read in
> 12-bit form within each and every "wedge".  Yes, multiple subsets of
> the table.  Notice also that the blocks chosen to hold these little
> tables could be impossible to write if they land over an actual bad
> spot!  [The main bad block table was written to the front of the media
> because that is more likely to be locally error-free; no such
> guarantee applies to each wedge's local bad block table sector.  Thus,
> any particular disk could flunk the wedge-mapper utility.]
> 
> The first responsibility of the handler is that for each and every
> sector to be transferred, you have to count up the total number of
> "potholes" that exist that are of lower logical sector number.  Thus,
> you now have the logical sector number of the one you now realize is
> the one you want, adjusted for all of those potholes that came before
> it.  [The highest logical sector in the wedge is the table block; you
> first have to read that in to establish the table; this is done each
> and every call, as there is insufficient code space room in the
> handler to guarantee you do/do not already have it read in.  Notice
> that inherently spare blocks are the ones just in front of the bad
> block table sector.]
> 
> Now that you know where this latest record is in logical terms of the
> wedge relative position, you have to translate that into the physical
> track it is located on, and calculate the difference between where you
> stored the latest known cylinder verified as having been read from,
> and the track where this desired sector is, and initiate a
> software-calculated seek operation [more on the RL01 magic jumper and
> this issue below].
> 
> Since there is no done flag, you have to monitor the heads-in-motion
> status until it is no longer moving, then perform a cylinder seek test
> to ensure it actually worked, or else the nuclear option of doing a
> total recal to recover [which all errors cause due to lack of room to
> only do constructive error recovery instead of brute force
> techniques].
> 
> Assuming you get this far, then you do the read or write in 12-bit
> mode [which wastes 1/4 of the drive!  Remember, all of the above waste
> nothing!  A 12-sector PDP-11 RK pack has 256 16-bit words per sector,
> but a 16-sector PDP-8 RK pack has 256 12-bit words per sector; the
> same exact data capacity].
> 
> Thus, treated totally as an island in itself, you have managed to
> transfer one sector or 256 PDP-8 12-bit words [or perhaps 1/2 of that
> if the last logical block of an odd-count handler call].
> 
> To access the next logical sector, you have to repeat everything!
> Within each "wedge" there is an inherent 2:1 interleave since it would
> be otherwise impossible to avoid a complete revolution of rotational
> latency.  Thus, it takes two revolutions of an entire disk to access
> sequentially all of the sectors of only one wedge, not all the sectors
> on the disk, since by definition most of them are always ignored!  And
> the ones that actually are involved, only contribute the equivalent of
> 3/4 of their size on any particular cylinder.  All effective transfers
> are wasting over 2/3 of the media in terms of getting to the next
> useful data, and only 3/4 of the relevant data is itself relevant.
> Compare this to all the other disks where no latency exists and all
> data is transferred and relevant and not wasted or partitioned out to
> another unrelated area of the disk.
> 
> On the RL01, you get a third half-size wedge, and on the RL02 you get
> 5 full-size wedges, all equally lame in terms of performance.
> 
> The difference between an RL01 and an RL02 is of course there are
> twice as many tracks on the RL02.  I do not know if the RL01 is
> supportable on the RL278; I rather doubt it.
> 
> To support the RL01 on the M8433 hex board, you have to set the
> "magic" jumper.  What this does is to ensure that the software seek
> register, which is a delta register with inherently a signed value in
> it, cannot exceed the RL01 physical architecture.  I assume this is
> because a lot of RL01 diagnostics and handlers were written without
> regard for reserving an additional bit of precision to the value
> calculated to ensure it would be ignoreable if allowed to be passed to
> the RL01 drive.  Note:  This is not merely a mask-off solvable by
> either using the jumper or a proper AND operation in all software
> [that in and of itself isn't trustworthy, but it's actually even
> worse].  This is a signed operation.  Thus, in RL01 mode, the lowest
> bit would have to consistent with the rest of the bits.  [It would
> essentially have to replicate the bit immediately more significent.
> For example, if the seek fix is to move exactly -1 tracks [one track
> back] the command to the disk register is to move one track back
> expressed as -1.  But if that but is untrustworthy, it might look like
> -2 if set wrong; thus the jumper is needed to ensure proper RL01
> operation.
> 
> I believe an RL01 pack can be read in an RL02 drive; the reverse is
> not true, and I do not know if an RL01 pack can be written in an RL02
> drive.  In any case, you have to set that magic jumper to get normal
> RL01 stuff to work in general, etc.
> 
> Thus, I doubt if RL01 can even be reliably read in the RL278 unless it
> actually has an equivalent to the jumper [which I doubt].
> 
> So, yes, the M8433 can be used as part of an RL01 or RL02 system, but
> to actually mix the drives and media, is harder than at first glance.
> 
> ... (non-RL stuff clipped) ...
> 
> Yes, this extends my scribblings out to the RL controller; I
> personally abhor it, and knew a lot of places really quite unhappy
> with it for obvious reasons.  The OS/8 support should never have been
> attempted; these drives easily get flakey unless handled with more
> precision and care than all the rest of the industry as well as all
> former DEC stuff.  Where I worked with the DSD-240, there never was a
> problem at all.
> 
> Look at how piss-poor it is on all systems, and then even worse on
> 12-bit mode transfers.
> 
> I could probably write a P?S/8 handler to get a lot closer to a good
> handler, but I must admit it might even tax P?S/8 if no compromise was
> required.  That would involve a resident copy of the master 8-bit bad
> block table, mapping what it indicates correctly within the scope of a
> handler, elimination of wedges, writing of code to avoid latency to
> the extent possible.  [It may not be possible to eliminate 2:1
> interleave, but it clearly might be able to make them apply to the
> entire track, not just a wedge.]  And it may be quite messy to get it
> to work in 8-bit mode as well.  Perhaps all but the latter is
> possible, still wasting 1/4 of the media.  But I would insist that
> proper error handling be the primary mission, not getting 8-bit mode
> to work.] [Note:  P?S/8 allows a full PDP-8 memory field-sized
> non-system handler.  In that much space, it can definitely be done,
> but this would still mean the ability to access "foreign" media
> because it wouldn't match the bootup format in 12-bit mode type disks
> that are essentially more like the OS/8 type sans wedges and
> wedge-resident baby error tables, etc.]
> 
> Clearly just mimicking the OS/8 code and wedge crap is easily done,
> and thus concentrate on the error recovery improvement, but it would
> still crawl; I don't think it's worth the effort to aim that low for
> P?S/8; I certainly can do a lot better, but only time can tell just
> how much better; it's a better use of my time to handle more "generic"
> problems than deal with this loser.
> 
> cjl
> -- 
> "In the future, OS/2 will be on everyone's desktop"
> 
> Bill Gates, 1992
> 


