I'll top-post this one...

cjl is, as usual, wordy, but very accurate.
The only thing I can add to this is that an RL02 drive cannot normally 
read an RL01 pack. When I worked at DEC, we had an RL02 which had been 
modified so it could read both RL01 packs and RL02 packs. I do not know 
exactly what the modification was, but we had a switch on the drive, 
which we flipped one way for RL01 and the other way for RL02 packs. So 
it would appear just a single signal somewhere in the drive needed to 
change, or be disabled, or something, in order to read the RL01 packs on 
an RL02 drive. Reading the schematics, I'm sure that modification could 
be recreated.

On seeks - DEC mus thave done a good brain fart there. Yes, seek 
complete do not generate any interrupt. The idea, it would appear, was 
that after a seek, you immediately initiated the transfer. The transfer 
was then suspended until the seek was complete. So, you would not have 
to worry about seek completes, and so on. The problem with all this is 
that it don't cover the potential of errors during the seek. Seeks can 
fail. Both in that you can have real errors, and also that seeks might 
not do the exact correct number of tracks, since it's just a track 
counter thing. After a seek, you need to verify that you are on the 
right track, and perhaps do another seek, if you missed the target track.
So in real life, you cannot do a seek followed immediately by a transfer.

*Sigh*

However, I have found RL02 drives to be very reliable in my personal 
experience. But then again, so are my RK05 drives...

	Johnny

