A few more notes from cjl on driver issues for RL02's:

> It's not a matter of when you do what and how quickly, the problem is that
> you have to be a slave to waiting for a status that doesn't generate an
> interrupt, AND the secondary problem that to avoid potential throughput
> lossage, you have to quickly initiate a transfer because the next sector to
> go by would otherwise be a wasted opportunity to be the one you want, and if
> you are in the midst of a multi-part transfer, you already know some of that
> for certain, thus you are not guessing that you really need to get the
> transfer going, you KNOW it.
> 
> Yet, you have to make high-response software make this a priority just to get
> this tidbit of information available quickly.   Since computer time
> immemorium, this has always been done via an interrupt, yet on this crap
> device, they didn't bother, thus all of the needless software complication on
> all versions.   On the -8, ANYTHING that causes overhead is unwelcome due to
> the unrealistic requirements of lame OS/8 handlers that get compromised due
> to merely the length of code it takes to accomplish anything, thus the
> problems compound each other.
> 
> It's just a matter of how bad a bad design compromises the rest of the
> software; it's always bad, just how much.   But merely talking about only one
> aspect of things undercuts the true stupidity of this device.
> 
> I think the small mod he refers to is a compatibillity mode jumper in the
> drive to make it emulate the lower density of the RL01 drive, so it would be
> a mandatory jumper to go back and forth.   But on the -8, that's not good
> enough as I mentioned because the full gamut of existing software is not
